+The plugin reads ONE environment per build variant out of
+`palbase/environments/<environment>/`, which is where `palbase link` writes that
+environment's `openapi.json` — which carries the role definitions inside itself,
+as `x-palbase-roles` — and its `android-config.json`. No `palbase { }` block is
+needed: the plugin reads the first of `<module>/palbase/environments` and
+`<checkout root>/palbase/environments` that exists.
+
+**The build type picks the environment.** Out of the box:
+
+| Build type | Compiles |
+|---|---|
+| `debug` | `local` |
+| `release` | nothing until you choose — the build FAILS and says how |
+| any other, e.g. `create("featureX")` | the environment of the same name, `palbase/environments/featureX/` |
+| `benchmarkRelease`, `nonMinifiedRelease` (baseline profiles) | whatever `release` compiles |
+
+Choose for a build type in ONE committed place — `gradle.properties`:
+
+```properties
+palbase.env.release=main
+```
+
+or the build type itself:
+
+```kotlin
+import io.palbase.gradle.palbase   // Kotlin DSL only; Groovy needs no import
+
+android {
+    buildTypes {
+        release { palbase { environment = "main" } }
+    }
+}
+```
+
+Without that import, a `palbase { }` inside a build type is the PROJECT's block
+and `environment` does not compile. `extensions.configure<io.palbase.gradle.PalbaseBuildType> { environment = "main" }`
+works with no import. If `gradle.properties` and the build type BOTH name an
+environment and they differ, the build fails rather than pick one.
+
+Aim your own build somewhere else without touching a committed file — in
+`local.properties`, or for one build on the command line (`<V>` is a variant
+such as `paidRelease`, `<B>` a build type; the variant key wins):
+
+```
+# local.properties
+palbase.env.debug=main
+```
 
 ```
-./gradlew assembleDebug -Ppalbase.env=main
+./gradlew assembleDebug -Ppalbase.env.debug=main
 ```
 
-Set it in `gradle.properties` to change the default for a checkout, or pass it
-per build; a CI job passes the environment it releases. A selected environment
-with no directory FAILS the build naming itself — nothing is ever generated from
-an environment that was not asked for.
+The full order, first hit wins: command line, `local.properties`, the build
+type's `palbase { environment }`, `gradle.properties`, the defaults above, and
+last the 2.3 global `palbase.env` (still honoured for `debug` and `release`).
+Every generation prints what it chose — `Palbase: release → main (palbase.env.release in gradle.properties)`.
+A chosen environment with no directory FAILS the build naming itself and where
+the name came from — nothing is ever generated from an environment that was not
+asked for. `initWith(…)` does not copy `palbase { environment }`.
 
 The plugin generates Kotlin sources per Android variant and packages the runtime
 config into the APK as `palbase/palbase-config.json`; no generated Kotlin needs
 to be committed. The app config must contain nonempty `app_id`, `base_url`, and
 `api_key` values written by the CLI.
 
